# A download from an earlier launch whose file is gone isn't a new download. When an extension lists the
# downloads (chrome.downloads.search), Chrome checks that each finished download's file is still on disk and
# updates the ones it can't find. Cra took that update for a download that had just finished: at every launch
# "Download finished" for old files (moved or deleted since), the download flight, and the same files added to
# downloads.json again (GitHub #519: two .mp4s from a week before, ten entries after a few launches).
# Here the fixture extension deletes the file it saw finish, and after the relaunch it searches as pages load.
# promise: #519
home
budget rss=600 stalls50=5
extension ${FIXTURES}/DownloadSearchExtension
cra://open?url=file://${FIXTURES}/Pages/downloads.html
expect-log loaded file:
cra://debug?cdp=Input.dispatchMouseEvent&params={"type":"mousePressed","x":36,"y":56,"button":"left","clickCount":1}
cra://debug?cdp=Input.dispatchMouseEvent&params={"type":"mouseReleased","x":36,"y":56,"button":"left","clickCount":1}
expect-log Download finished from a data: address
wait 2
relaunch
# Chrome checks the files at most every 10 s, and the first search may come before the profile's
# history has loaded.
wait 11
cra://open?url=${PAGE}?n=first
wait 3
cra://open?url=${PAGE}?n=second
expect-log GET /page.html\?n=downloads-1-gone-1
cra://debug?downloadState=relaunched
expect-log download state relaunched: .* finished=0$
